# core.autocrlf=true is set at system scope on the Windows boxes this repository is
# developed on, so a plain checkout writes CRLF into every file git touches. Two things
# then break, both quietly.
#
# gofmt reports every Go file as unformatted, because a carriage return is not what it
# would have written — so `gofmt -l` stops distinguishing a real formatting error from a
# fresh clone, and the usual response is to stop running it.
#
# And a gate that matches on the text of a source file matches nothing at all. That is
# not a gate failing; it is a gate reporting the clean run of a complete gate having read
# nothing, which is the single most expensive failure mode this project has. It has
# already happened here once, to 84 source anchors that passed vacuously on Windows.
#
# connect pins its protobuf inputs and their generated Go for the same reason. Here it is
# the whole of the Go source and the module files beside it.
*.go text eol=lf
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf

# The documents go the same way, and this line is the one spec B §13 item 28 names verbatim —
# TestGitattributesCarriesTheLineSpecB13Item28Names reads it back out of item 28 rather than
# out of a copy here, so the two cannot drift. working-tree-encoding pins the documents to
# UTF-8 in the working tree as well as in the index, which is the half that matters: the
# mojibake item 28 hunts for is produced by an editor that read a UTF-8 file as cp1252 and
# wrote it back, and git will now refuse the round trip instead of storing it.
*.md text working-tree-encoding=UTF-8 eol=lf

# release-platforms.txt is parsed by deps_test.go and by the release job, and gates.yml by
# nothing that tolerates a stray carriage return either.
*.txt text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
